import json,pathlib
r='frontier-40-geometry-braids-rep-27';p=pathlib.Path('research')/f'{r}-batch-25.pages.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());src={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, 54.8.8 and 54.11.6: exact imports replaced by the local argument','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'}
def row(id,title,deps,statement,strategy,level=0):return dict(id=id,kind='lemma',title=title,deps=deps,statement=statement,strategy=strategy,design_row='AG-BIR-1',local_addition=True,provenance={'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},sources={'references':[src]},dependency_level=level)
h1=row('lem-finite-regular-base-algebra-dualizing-biduality','Dualizing biduality for finite algebras over a regular base',['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dualizing-complex-on-projective-cm-scheme','lem-finite-closed-immersion-derived-coinduction-adjunction','thm-localisation-and-polynomial-extension-of-regular-rings','lem-global-dimension-is-detected-on-cyclic-modules'],r'''Assume AC. Let $R$ be a regular Noetherian ring of finite dimension $d$, and let $B\ne0$ be a module-finite $R$-algebra. For any integer $s$, $D_B=R\operatorname{Hom}_R(B,R[s])$ is a dualizing complex over $B$. For every $M\in D^b_{\mathrm{fin}}(B)$ the canonical evaluation $M\to R\operatorname{Hom}_B(R\operatorname{Hom}_B(M,D_B),D_B)$ is an isomorphism; both duals are bounded with finite cohomology. These constructions and their evaluations commute with localization on $R$ and $B$.''',r'''Every finite $R$-module has a bounded finite-projective resolution, by the finite global-dimension bound and Noetherian finite syzygies; truncation triangles give the same for bounded finite complexes. The invertible rank-one module $R$ has injective dimension at most $d$ by the global/injective dimension supplier. Resolve $R[s]$ by bounded injectives and apply $\operatorname{Hom}_R(B,-)$, which consists of injective $B$-modules by finite-ring coinduction. Thus $D_B$ has finite injective dimension; finite-projective resolutions of $B$ give finite cohomology. For a bounded finite $B$-complex $M$, coinduction gives $R\operatorname{Hom}_B(M,D_B)=R\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,R[s])$ after forgetting $B$. The latter is bounded finite, since $M$ is finite over $R$. Applying the adjunction again identifies its double dual with the termwise double dual of a finite-projective $R$-resolution of $M$, canonically isomorphic to $M$. Evaluation under adjunction is the same signed termwise evaluation (at $1\in B$), so it is the actual $B$-bidual map; forgetting scalars detects quasi-isomorphisms. At $M=B$ this proves the homothety isomorphism. The finite resolutions prove compatibility with localization; the same argument localized on an affine $B$-open gives the asserted local dualizing conditions. AC is inherited from resolutions. This extends the quotient-only supplier's proof to finite algebras without treating that supplier as already having the broader statement.''')
h2=row('lem-finite-length-duality-over-a-regular-local-base','Finite-length duality over a regular local base',['def-axiom-of-choice',h1['id'],'cor-koszul-complex-resolves-a-regular-quotient','thm-regular-local-rings-are-domains-and-cohen-macaulay','thm-long-exact-ext-sequence-in-the-first-variable'],r'''Assume AC. Let $(R,\mathfrak m)$ be regular Noetherian local of dimension $d$ and let $(B,\mathfrak n)$ be a module-finite local $R$-algebra with the map local. For finite-length $B$-modules put $T(M)=\operatorname{Ext}_R^d(M,R)$ with its natural $B$-action. Then $\operatorname{Ext}_R^i(M,R)=0$ for $i\ne d$, $T$ is exact contravariantly, $M\cong T(T(M))$ by canonical bidual evaluation, and $\operatorname{Ann}_B T(M)=\operatorname{Ann}_B M$.''',r'''A finite-length $B$-module has finite length over $R$: the residue extension is finite, and a $B$-composition series has factors finite-dimensional over $R/\mathfrak m$. A regular system of parameters of $R$ is a regular sequence generating $\mathfrak m$. Its Koszul resolution and its signed self-duality show $\operatorname{Ext}_R^i(R/\mathfrak m,R)=0$ except $i=d$, where it is $R/\mathfrak m$. Induct on an $R$-composition series of $M$, using the long exact Ext sequence, to get concentration in degree $d$ and exactness of $T$. Apply the preceding finite-base bidual lemma with $B$ and shift $s=d$: finite-ring adjunction identifies $R\operatorname{Hom}_B(M,D_B)$ with $R\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,R[d])$, which is precisely $T(M)$ in degree zero. Applying the adjunction a second time gives $M\cong T(T(M))$, as a $B$-module via the same evaluation. Multiplication by $b\in B$ on $T(M)$ is obtained contravariantly from multiplication on $M$; thus if it vanishes on $M$ it vanishes on $T(M)$, and conversely apply $T$ again and use bidual evaluation. Hence the annihilators agree. This supplies the exact finite-length duality and annihilator detection used in Stacks 54.8.8, without asserting general Matlis duality.''',1)
h3=row('lem-finite-birational-algebra-descends-from-a-flat-completion-neighbourhood','Finite birational algebras descend across a flat completion neighbourhood',['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dependent-choice','lem-finite-local-modules-admit-minimal-free-resolutions','thm-yoneda-ext-one-is-naturally-isomorphic-to-derived-ext-one','thm-faithfully-flat-ring-map-characterisations','cor-faithfully-flat-ring-maps-are-injective','thm-localisation-of-modules-is-exact'],r'''Assume AC and DC. Let $B\to C$ be a faithfully flat map of Noetherian domains and let $0\ne I\subset B$ be a finitely generated ideal such that $B/I^n\to C/I^nC$ is an isomorphism for every $n\ge1$. Let $C\subset C'$ be a finite birational algebra inside $\operatorname{Frac}C$, with $C'/C$ annihilated by a power of $IC$. Then there is a finite birational algebra $B\subset B'\subset\operatorname{Frac}B$, isomorphic to $B$ away from $V(I)$, and an isomorphism $B'\otimes_B C\cong C'$ as $C$-algebras.''',r'''Put $Q=C'/C$ and choose $n$ with $I^nQ=0$. Via $B/I^n=C/I^nC$, $Q$ is a finite $B$-module and $Q\otimes_BC=Q$. Resolve $Q$ by degreewise finite free $B$-modules; Noetherianity supplies each finite syzygy. Flatness and degreewise finite Hom give $\operatorname{Ext}_B^1(Q,B)\otimes_BC\cong\operatorname{Ext}_C^1(Q,C)$. The left Ext group is annihilated by $I^n$: multiplication on a projective resolution lifting a scalar that kills $Q$ is chain homotopic to zero, by successive projective lifts, and induces zero on Hom cohomology. Every module killed by $I^n$ is unchanged by tensoring with $C$, so the displayed map is itself an isomorphism. The class of $0\to C\to C'\to Q\to0$ therefore lifts to an extension $0\to B\to E\to Q\to0$; Ext-one classification gives an endpoint-preserving isomorphism $E\otimes_BC\cong C'$. The extension makes $E$ finite. Choose $0\ne a\in I$. Since $Q_a=0$, $E_a=B_a$; the map $E\to E_a$ is injective, as after the faithful flat base change any $a$-power torsion would vanish in the domain $C'$. Thus identify $E$ with a $B$-submodule of $B_a\subset\operatorname{Frac}B$ containing $B$. Its base change embeds in $B_a\otimes_BC=C_a$ and identifies there with $C'$, by the endpoint-preserving isomorphism localized at $a$. For $u,v\in E$, the product $uv\in B_a$ maps into $C'=E\otimes C$. The class of $uv$ in $(B_a/E)\otimes C$ is zero; faithful flatness makes the original class zero. Hence $uv\in E$. Give $B'=E$ this induced ring structure: its unit is the image of $1\in B$, and base change has exactly the ring structure of $C'$. The quotient is $Q$, so $B'=B$ away from $V(I)$. DC is retained from Ext-one classification and AC from degreewise finite resolutions. No abstract formal-gluing theorem is imported.''')
